course: biology
lesson: 393
title: Mushrooms and creatures to avoid in the woods: death caps, adders, ticks and hornets
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A walk in the woods is full of treasures: mushrooms pushing up through the leaves, a snake sunning itself on the path, a deep buzz from a hollow tree. Almost everything there is harmless if you look with your eyes and keep your hands to yourself.
explain: A few things need respect. The deadliest mushroom in the world, the death cap, looks plain and tastes fine. The adder bites only when stepped on or grabbed. A tiny tick can hitch a ride on your leg without you feeling it.
explain: None of these creatures is out to get you: the adder would rather slide away, the hornet only defends its nest, and the boar avoids people. Trouble comes when people grab, poke, step on or come too close.
explain: So the woods rules are simple: never pick and eat mushrooms without an expert, never touch snakes, nests or hairy caterpillars, walk calmly away, check your skin after long grass, and tell a grown-up about anything that bites or stings.
fact: The death cap, a plain pale green-yellow mushroom often found under oaks, causes most deaths from mushroom poisoning in the world.
fact: The death cap has no bad taste, and the illness starts many hours later, when the poison has already harmed the liver; cooking, freezing or drying does not make it safe.
fact: The fly agaric, red with white spots like in cartoons and Mario games, is real and poisonous and grows in birch and pine woods; its white spots are bits of the skin that wrapped the young mushroom and can wash off in the rain.
fact: Silver spoons turning black, onions changing colour and slugs nibbling a mushroom are all myths and prove nothing; many poisonous mushrooms show no sign, and only an expert who knows each kind can tell.
fact: The mushroom is only the fruit of a fungus whose threads live in the soil; the thin gills under the cap drop billions of tiny spores, enough to make a print on paper.
fact: The adder, a grey-brown snake with a dark zigzag down its back, is venomous and lives in Ukraine and much of Europe, but it bites only when stepped on or grabbed and would rather slide away.
fact: Adder bites are rare and seldom deadly with hospital care; anyone bitten must keep still and get a grown-up and a doctor quickly.
fact: The grass snake, with two yellow patches behind its head, is harmless, loves water and frogs, and defends itself by hissing, giving off a bad smell or playing dead; it is still best to leave all snakes alone.
fact: A tick is a tiny eight-legged relative of spiders, not an insect; it waits on grass tips, grabs passing animals and drinks blood, and some ticks carry illnesses like Lyme disease.
fact: A grown-up should remove a tick with a tick tool and watch the spot; if a red ring appears, a doctor should see it.
fact: The European hornet is the biggest wasp in Ukraine and is usually calmer than small wasps; it stings only if its nest is disturbed or it is squashed or grabbed, so walk calmly away from nests.
fact: Some hairy caterpillars have tiny hairs that break off and make skin itch and burn, and the hairs can float in the air near their silky nests, so look with your eyes only.
fact: Wild boar dig up the forest floor with their snouts for roots and worms; they usually avoid people, but a mother with stripy piglets may charge, so go back the way you came and never get between a mother and her young.
exercise: Which mushroom causes most deaths from mushroom poisoning in the world? | The death cap | The fly agaric | The field mushroom | The puffball
exercise: Why is the death cap so dangerous? | It tastes fine and the illness starts many hours later | It smells so bad that people faint just from walking past it in the woods | It bites the fingers of people who pick it | It explodes into a cloud of poison when touched
exercise: Does cooking a death cap make it safe? | No, cooking, freezing or drying does not help | Yes, if you boil it hard in salty water for a whole hour first | Yes, if you fry it well in butter | Yes, if you dry it out in the sun for a week
exercise: What are the white spots on a fly agaric? | Bits of the skin that wrapped the young mushroom | Drops of white poison that slowly leak out of the red cap | Tiny eggs laid on the cap by forest flies | Paint added by mushroom collectors
exercise: Does a silver spoon turning black show a mushroom is poisonous? | No, it is a myth and proves nothing | Yes, it always works, which is why cooks keep silver spoons | Yes, but only with very old spoons | Only for the red mushrooms
exercise: Who can tell a safe mushroom from a poisonous one? | Only an expert who knows each kind | Anyone who checks the colour of the cap carefully | Anyone who sees slugs eating it | Anyone with a silver spoon
exercise: What do the gills under a mushroom cap hold? | Billions of tiny spores | Water for the dry season | Seeds like an apple | Tiny insects that guard it
exercise: Where does most of a fungus live? | As threads in the soil | Inside the mushroom cap | In the air as a cloud | Under the bark of every tree
exercise: When does an adder usually bite? | When it is stepped on or grabbed | Whenever it sees a person | Only at night while hunting people | When people sing near it
exercise: What should someone bitten by an adder do? | Keep still and get a grown-up and a doctor quickly | Run home as fast as possible so the poison is sweated out | Try to catch the snake and take it to the doctor | Ignore it, adder bites are nothing
exercise: How can you tell a grass snake from an adder? | The grass snake has two yellow patches behind its head | The grass snake has a dark zigzag down its back | The grass snake is always bright red | The grass snake has legs
exercise: How does a grass snake defend itself? | Hissing, a bad smell or playing dead | A strong venomous bite, like an adder | Spitting poison into the eyes of its enemy | Squeezing people until they faint
exercise: Is a tick an insect? | No, it has eight legs and is a relative of spiders | Yes, it has six legs and a hard shell, just like a little beetle | Yes, it is a very small kind of fly | No, it is a tiny snail that lives in grass
exercise: Some days after a tick bite a red ring appears around the spot. What should happen? | A doctor should see it | Nothing, red rings are normal | Scratch it until it fades | Cover it with mud
exercise: When does a European hornet sting? | When its nest is disturbed or it is squashed or grabbed | Whenever it sees a person anywhere, even far away from its nest | Only when it is very hungry and cannot find food | Every time it flies past someone
exercise: Why should you never touch hairy caterpillars or their webs? | Their hairs break off and make skin itch and burn | They bite the ends of your fingers off with their jaws | They are made of a sticky glue that never comes off your hands | They turn into hornets at once
exercise: You see a wild boar mother with stripy piglets. What should you do? | Stay calm and go back the way you came | Run between her and the piglets | Try to stroke a piglet | Throw sticks to chase her away
test: In Mario games the red mushroom with white spots makes you grow. What is true of the real fly agaric? | It is poisonous, so never eat it | It makes children grow taller | It is the tastiest wild mushroom | It is only found in cartoons
test: «grandad's mate says slugs nibbled it so it's safe, chuck it in the soup» What is the best reply? | That is a myth; slugs eat poisonous mushrooms too, only an expert can tell | Right, slugs can smell poison, so they only ever eat the mushrooms that are safe | Right, slugs taste food for us | Only if two slugs nibbled it
test: A plain pale mushroom under an oak tastes fine and nobody feels ill for hours. Why is this the worst kind? | By the time illness starts, the poison has already harmed the liver | Mild-tasting mushrooms are always safe, so nothing bad can be happening at all | A late illness is always a weak one | Slow poisons wash out by themselves
test: «there's a snake on the path, chuck a stick at it!» What is the best thing to do? | Stand still, let it slide away, or go round it at a distance | Throw sticks at it until it moves off the path and into the grass | Pick it up by the tail and put it to one side | Stamp near it to scare it
test: What would happen if you stepped on an adder lying in the sun? | It might bite to defend itself | It would always chase you home | It would stay still and let you pass | It would spit poison into the air
test: «it's just a grass snake, it's played dead, poke it» What is the best advice? | Leave all snakes alone, even harmless ones | Poke it, grass snakes like being prodded | Pick it up and take it home as a pet | Turn it over to check it is dead
test: Count them: mum finds 2 grown-up ticks on the dog. How many legs is that, and are they insects? | 16 legs, and no, ticks are not insects | 12 legs, and yes, they are insects | 16 legs, and yes, they are insects | 12 legs, and no, ticks are not insects
test: «was in long grass all arvo, so what» Why should you check your skin? | Tick bites usually do not hurt, so ticks are often found only later | Long grass leaves sharp splinters stuck in the skin of every leg | Long grass turns your skin green | Ticks make a buzzing sound to warn you, so you would have heard them
test: A huge orange-and-brown wasp buzzes past. Your friend starts waving his arms. Why is walking away better? | Hornets sting when they feel attacked, and calm walking does not threaten them | Waving your arms about is the only thing that makes hornets fall asleep in the air | Hornets cannot see people who walk | Hornets only sting people who stand still
test: In a cartoon a boy whacks a hornets' nest with a stick and a swarm chases him. What should he really do near a nest? | Never poke or throw things at it, walk calmly away | Hit it once more, harder, to finish the nest off for good | Cover it with his coat so the hornets cannot get out | Shout to scare the hornets off
test: «found a boar hole in the woods, gonna go see if the piglets are about» What is the problem? | A mother with piglets may charge to protect them, so go back the way you came | Wild boars only live in zoos, so it must be some other animal | Piglets bite much harder than adults | Nothing, boars love company and will come and say hello
test: Count them: a ranger shows 3 mushrooms and asks which kinds are safe to pick. What is the safe answer for a child? | None, until an expert checks each one | All 3, they look plain | The 2 that look most like shop mushrooms | Only the brightest one
exam: Identify the mushroom responsible for the greatest number of deaths from mushroom poisoning throughout the world. | The death cap | The fly agaric | The field mushroom | The common puffball
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning traditional tests for poisonous mushrooms, such as the blackening of a silver spoon. | Such tests are without foundation, and only an expert familiar with each species may distinguish them | Such tests are reliable in all cases, the blackening of silver being caused by the poison itself | Such tests are reliable for red mushrooms exclusively | Such tests are reliable provided that the spoon is of great age
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the tick? | It is an eight-legged relative of spiders which feeds upon blood and may transmit Lyme disease | It is a six-legged insect which feeds exclusively upon leaves | It is a winged insect which stings in defence of its nest and which is found chiefly in the roofs of houses | It is a small snail which inhabits long grass
exam: Ascertain the circumstances in which the adder is ordinarily disposed to bite a human being. | When it is trodden upon or seized | Whenever a human being is observed at a distance | Exclusively at night, in pursuit of human beings | When a human being speaks in its vicinity
